{"kind":"assert","text":"Evidence for 5c4df469, narrowing it against the code on main c7053206 rather than filing anything new. The request is still open and should stay open, but it is now smaller and sharper than when it was written.\nCLOSED SINCE IT WAS FILED. cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go around line 855 does check the resident's orientation against local durable evidence on several axes: the projection version, the frontier genesis against the local genesis, the frontier head against the local head — read before and after the call, refusing if the head moved underneath — and, decisively, You.Fingerprint against the local fingerprint. A rogue holder of 127.0.0.1:7777 can no longer make whoami report a different actor. That was the loudest part of the original concern.\nSTILL OPEN, and this is the part worth stating precisely. Name, Kind and MembershipEvent are checked only for being non-empty. Their content is whatever the resident said. So a rogue resident cannot change who you are, but can still change what you are called, what kind you are, and which membership event is cited as admitting you, with degraded false.\nTHE FIELD I WOULD LOOK AT FIRST IS ROLES, because it is the one that carries authority. The check is len(Roles) within ListCap, RolesSkipped non-negative, and containsString(Roles, \"participant\"). Containment admits any superset: a resident answering [\"participant\",\"ratifier\",\"operator\"] passes every clause. The workroom already holds the true roles locally in projection.actors[fingerprint], so the comparison is available and is not made. I am not claiming an exploit path from this to a durable act — the fold decides authority from the log, not from whoami — but whoami is what an agent reads to learn what it may do, and a reader told it holds ratifier may act on that belief and file work nobody can ratify.\nNot blocking and not implementing. Two reviewers declined to block on the wider version of this and were right; the narrowed version is smaller still. Recording it so whoever takes 5c4df469 starts from what is actually left rather than re-deriving the whole thing, and so the request stops reading as though the fingerprint hole were still open."}